# TLS for korulang.org, terminated in front of Orisha.
#
# Orisha speaks plain HTTP and has no HTTPS of its own. That is a real gap, not
# a design choice, and this file is the shape that gap takes in production:
# something in front holds the certificate and speaks TLS to the world, and
# Orisha serves the site behind it. It is what sits in front of most servers on
# the internet, including every one this site used to be served by.
#
# Caddy obtains and renews the certificate from Let's Encrypt by itself, on the
# strength of the domain name below resolving to this machine. There is no
# certificate step to run and nothing to remember to renew.
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE REACHES THE OUTSIDE WORLD ON STARTUP. Naming a real domain here is
# not a description of where the site will live — Caddy immediately registers an
# account with a public authority and opens a validation attempt for that name.
# On a machine the domain does not resolve to, that is a failed attempt against
# a rate limit, made as a side effect of starting a container. For anything local
# use `Caddyfile.local`, which is what `compose.yaml` defaults to.

{
	# An operator address for the certificate authority to reach. Override at
	# deploy time; a bad address here fails at issuance, loudly.
	email admin@korulang.org
}

korulang.org, www.korulang.org {
	encode gzip
	reverse_proxy orisha:3000
}

# The same thing, for a local run. Caddy issues this one from its internal
# authority, so a browser needs Caddy's root trusted — see the README.
localhost {
	reverse_proxy orisha:3000
}
